    {
      "id": "berklee-music-theory-book-1-p0039",
      "source_en": "Lesson 16. Enharmonic Equivalents\n\nWhen notes are spelled differently (by a different letter), but have the same pitch and are the same key on the keyboard, they are known as enharmonic equivalents, or just enharmonics. For example, a half step up from F is F#, and a half step down from G is Gb. Note that F# and Gb are the same physical key on the keyboard. They are enharmonic equivalents. Also note that E and F, and B and C are a half step apart; there are no half steps between them.\n\nHere are some enharmonic equivalents:\n\nPractice\n\n1. Circle the measures that contain enharmonic equivalents.",
